WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
[rhythmic tap sounds]

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:09.240
Tap is one of the first original American art forms.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:15.080
[rhythmic tap sounds]

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:19.660
It’s an instantly recognizable combination of movement and sound.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:26.240
Jane Goldberg: It’s such a fun thing to see because people wants to see what’s in the bottom of the shoe.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:30.380
Jane: How you make the sound, that’s always a curiosity.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:35.920
Jane: This is how we made the sound, with the aluminum. Originally they were wooden.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:40.700
Tap dancing started in Five Points. Now Chinatown in New York City.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:44.260
This slum was one of the city's first melting pots.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:50.520
The percussive dances that nurtured tap, came from african ceremonies, irish jigging and british clogging.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:56.140
In the early 1800s, African-American and Irish people lived and worked side by side.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:00.300
Martin Scorsese referenced the origins of tap in Gangs of New York.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:16.940
Some historians who believe that enslaved people in America started using their bodies and feet for percussion after drums were banned to discourage rebellion;

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.400
that part of the history is shared with jazz.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:25.700
And with improvisation as an essential element, tap is also considered jazz.

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:32.220
Jane: We were considered musicians. We considered ourselves, musicians.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:40.180
Historical records from the mid 1800s describe early tap as a popular entertainment for blacks and whites in cellars and dance halls.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:43.440
Minstrels performed along with folk music on violins and banjos.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:49.480
Those Irish reels were the pop music at the time, and as pop music evolved, so did the fancy footwork.

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:56.260
Early tappers, like Master Juba, even went on tour to Europe.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:05.300
By the end of that century, black minstrel shows made people laugh and, over time, tap dancing became associated with American comedy.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:11.020
In the 1900s, tap appeared in films even before they had sound.

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:17.820
The style became very popular in the 1930s and ‘40s, when it was featured in major Hollywood movies and Broadway shows.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:29.760
As the decades passed tap was displaced by other styles in popular music and was relegated to musical theater.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.900
Traditional tap became an underground expression.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:40.020
In the 70s in New York, Jane Goldberg and Brenda Bufalino produced shows to return the old hoofers back to the stage.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:49.360
Jane: It really was a movement and i think that we were looking for authenticity. And we knew that the Broadway Tap wasn’t, quote, “the real thing.”

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:00.180
Jane: We don’t go 5, 6, 7, 8. We go, uh uh uh uh. You know in other words,

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:02.900
Jane: You don’t count, just do it rhythmically.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:11.240
The style is still strong in New York City, where young dancers honor the old hoofers.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:21.120
Jason Bernard: To make music from your feet, from your ancestors and your spirit to come out that way. I think it’s quite amazing.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:28.700
Jason: I would say that the most important thing is the music. When the music isn’t there anymore.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:35.040
Jason:  And this is being substituted say via the arms or the face, or not being honest...

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:39.120
Jason: that’s when it becomes a problem. It’s all about the music.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:48.440
There are old moves still in force. A basic routine from the 1920s the shim sham is still danced worldwide.

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:55.620
And tap moves on, merging with new immigrant styles.

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:05.780
Felipe Galganni: Always felt very connected with the rhythms that I grew up with.  And so I decided to just put it together with my other passion, tap dance.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:11.520
Felipe: ...Bossa nova, samba, maracatu, all those rhythms together.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:16.560
Felipe: I mix the American art form—tap dance—with my Brazilian influence.

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:26.820
As tap evolves, dancers who preserve the old hoofer style are hopeful that it will continue to capture audiences into the future.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:34.520
Jason: Something that is so intimate and so rare. I believe is always fresh, always new.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:39.700
Jason: Whenever people see tap dancing, they say, "Wow! How can you do that with your feet?"

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:42.440
Jason: It just always makes people smile.

